---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import ExperimentPipeline from "../../components/ExperimentPipeline.astro";
import FenExplorer from "../../components/FenExplorer.astro";
import ModelResults from "../../components/ModelResults.astro";

const title = "Distilling Chess Intelligence · Nikolas Marinkovich";
const description = "Testing whether compact neural networks can reproduce how Leela Chess Zero evaluates chess positions.";
const technologies = ["Python", "PyTorch", "NumPy", "python-chess", "Leela Chess Zero"];
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description}>
  <Header />
  <main id="top" tabindex="-1">
    <header class="case-hero container">
      <a class="back-link" href="/#work"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Selected work</a>
      <div class="case-hero-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Undergraduate thesis · 2024</p>
          <h1>Distilling Chess Intelligence</h1>
          <p class="case-subtitle">Testing whether small neural networks can reproduce how Leela Chess Zero evaluates chess positions.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="case-meta">
          <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Machine-learning research and implementation</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Institution</dt><dd>University of Toronto Engineering Science</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Guidance</dt><dd><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~guerzhoy/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Professor Michael Guerzhoy</a></dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <ul class="tag-list" aria-label="Project technologies">{technologies.map((technology) => <li>{technology}</li>)}</ul>
    </header>

    <section class="wide-section container" aria-label="Experiment overview">
      <ExperimentPipeline />
    </section>

    <article class="case-body">
      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="question-title">
        <p class="section-number">01 · Research question</p>
        <h2 id="question-title">Can a small model learn how a chess engine sees the board?</h2>
        <p class="lede">Chess combines simple rules with a search space large enough to demand sophisticated evaluation, planning, and pattern recognition. I wanted to investigate what makes a strong chess engine effective.</p>
        <p>My undergraduate thesis began with a broad question: why did computers surpass the strongest human chess players years before achieving the same milestone in Go? Search-tree size explains part of the difference, but not how difficult a position is to evaluate without deep search.</p>
        <p>With theoretical guidance from <a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~guerzhoy/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Professor Michael Guerzhoy</a>, I narrowed that question into a practical experiment: how much of a strong chess engine’s positional judgment could be transferred into a much smaller neural network?</p>
      </section>

      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
        <p class="section-number">02 · Experiment</p>
        <h2 id="experiment-title">Turning the question into a teacher–student system.</h2>
        <p>Modern neural-network chess engines combine position evaluation with tree search. My experiment isolated the evaluation problem by treating Leela Chess Zero, or LC0, as a teacher model. Instead of asking a smaller model to select moves or play complete games, I trained it to predict LC0’s numerical evaluation of a board position.</p>
        <p>This is a form of knowledge distillation: a capable model produces targets that smaller models attempt to approximate. Success would suggest that useful positional knowledge could be represented compactly; failure would help characterize the limits of simple architectures.</p>
        <aside class="ownership-callout"><strong>My contribution</strong><p>I wrote the experiment code, generated the dataset, trained the models, and analyzed the results. <a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~guerzhoy/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Professor Guerzhoy</a> provided theoretical guidance and helped define the experimental direction.</p></aside>
      </section>

      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="data-title">
        <p class="section-number">03 · Data pipeline</p>
        <h2 id="data-title">15,000 teacher-labelled positions.</h2>
        <p>I sourced public chess positions represented in Forsyth–Edwards Notation, configured LC0 locally, and evaluated each position programmatically. LC0 received a ten-second evaluation window and returned a score in centipawns, where 100 centipawns corresponds approximately to one pawn.</p>
        <div class="inline-metrics">
          <div><strong>15,000</strong><span>positions evaluated</span></div>
          <div><strong>10 sec</strong><span>per teacher evaluation</span></div>
          <div><strong>~42 h</strong><span>evaluation budget</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>The resulting workflow read each position, requested an LC0 score from White’s perspective, converted the board into a tensor, and stored the input with its teacher target for training.</p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <section class="feature-section">
      <div class="container"><FenExplorer /></div>
    </section>

    <article class="case-body">
      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="representation-title">
        <p class="section-number">04 · Representation</p>
        <h2 id="representation-title">A smaller encoding with the same essential board information.</h2>
        <p>I tested a 12-channel representation with a separate channel for every piece-and-colour combination and a smaller seven-channel representation with six piece-type channels plus one colour channel. Neither showed a meaningful generalization advantage, so I retained the smaller 8 × 8 × 7 tensor.</p>
        <p>The encoding captured piece type, colour, and location, but not higher-level concepts such as king safety, pawn structure, mobility, tactical threats, or game history. The models had to infer useful patterns from this spatial input and LC0’s scores.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="models-title">
        <p class="section-number">05 · Student architectures</p>
        <h2 id="models-title">Nine models with increasing capacity.</h2>
        <p>The comparison included one linear baseline, four Conv2D configurations, and four Conv3D configurations. The linear model acted like a learned piece-and-square valuation system; the convolutional models were intended to capture increasingly complex spatial relationships.</p>
        <p>I trained on approximately 12,000 positions and evaluated against 3,000 held-out positions using L1 loss, interpreted as mean absolute error in centipawns.</p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <section class="results-section">
      <div class="container"><ModelResults /></div>
    </section>

    <article class="case-body">
      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <p class="section-number">06 · Conclusion</p>
        <h2 id="conclusion-title">Learning the training set was not enough.</h2>
        <p>The linear baseline achieved a held-out mean absolute error of 205.10 centipawns. Conv2D models ranged from 215.06 to 232.96, while Conv3D models ranged from 206.73 to 215.16. None consistently improved on the baseline.</p>
        <aside class="conclusion-callout"><p>Under this dataset, representation, training procedure, and family of shallow architectures, the convolutional students did not approximate LC0 better than a simple linear baseline on held-out positions.</p></aside>
        <p>This does not prove that compact chess evaluation is impossible. The experiment was constrained by dataset size, compute, model selection, and the way positions were divided. Predicting a teacher’s centipawn score also measures similarity to that teacher, not playing strength.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="retrospective-title">
        <p class="section-number">07 · Retrospective</p>
        <h2 id="retrospective-title">What I would change today.</h2>
        <p>I would begin with a substantially larger dataset and a reproducible game-level split. The source file stored consecutive positions from games, so complete games should be assigned between training, validation, and testing with a fixed seed. Multiple seeds would show how stable each result was.</p>
        <p>I would add constant and material-based baselines, expand the architecture search, examine the teacher-score distribution more carefully, and measure both prediction error and playing strength by placing each learned evaluator inside a search procedure.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="prose-section" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
        <p class="section-number">08 · Reflection</p>
        <h2 id="reflection-title">The result changed how I design ML experiments.</h2>
        <p>The part of this project I value most is the end-to-end system behind the experiments. I sourced the positions, configured LC0, generated the teacher-labelled dataset, built two board encodings, implemented the model families, and evaluated the results.</p>
        <p>The distillation itself was not successful, but the project made three lessons concrete: lower training loss is not the same as learning a useful representation; more complexity is not automatically better; and negative results become valuable when the experiment is scoped carefully enough to explain what failed.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="project-links" aria-labelledby="links-title">
        <div><p class="section-number">Project sources</p><h2 id="links-title">Explore the academic artifact.</h2></div>
        <div>
          <div class="source-links">
            <a href="https://github.com/Niko1909/Thesis-2024" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Thesis repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a href="https://lczero.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Leela Chess Zero <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a href="https://python-chess.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">python-chess <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
          <p>The repository reflects the original academic project. Reproducibility and presentation cleanup are planned as a separate phase.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Case study navigation"><a href="/#work"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to selected work</a><a href="mailto:niko.marinkovich@gmail.com">Discuss this project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-hero { padding-block: clamp(64px, 9vw, 112px) 72px; }
  .back-link { display: inline-flex; gap: 10px; align-items: center; color: var(--muted); font-size: .86rem; }
  .case-hero-grid { margin-top: 56px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.55fr .65fr; gap: clamp(48px, 8vw, 112px); align-items: end; }
  .case-hero h1 { max-width: 820px; margin: 0; font-size: clamp(3.2rem, 7.5vw, 6.6rem); font-weight: 500; line-height: .95; letter-spacing: -.065em; }
  .case-subtitle { max-width: 760px; margin: 30px 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); line-height: 1.5; }
  .case-meta { margin: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-meta div { padding: 14px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); } .case-meta dt { color: var(--accent); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .62rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; } .case-meta dd { margin: 4px 0 0; font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .case-hero .tag-list { margin-top: 40px; }
  .wide-section { padding-bottom: 112px; }
  .case-body { width: min(calc(100% - 48px), 760px); margin-inline: auto; }
  .prose-section { padding-block: 88px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .section-number { margin: 0 0 18px; color: var(--accent); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .prose-section h2, .project-links h2 { margin: 0 0 28px; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3.4rem); font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -.05em; }
  .prose-section > p { margin: 20px 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.03rem; } .prose-section .lede { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .ownership-callout, .conclusion-callout { margin: 36px 0 0; padding: 24px 28px; background: var(--accent-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); }
  .ownership-callout strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; } .ownership-callout p, .conclusion-callout p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }
  .conclusion-callout p { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.13rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .inline-metrics { margin: 36px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .inline-metrics div { padding: 24px 14px; border-right: 1px solid var(--border); } .inline-metrics div:last-child { border: 0; } .inline-metrics strong, .inline-metrics span { display: block; } .inline-metrics strong { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.5rem; } .inline-metrics span { margin-top: 5px; color: var(--muted); font-size: .72rem; line-height: 1.4; }
  .feature-section { padding-block: 112px; background: var(--accent-soft); }
  .results-section { padding-block: 112px; background: var(--surface); }
  .project-links { margin-top: 88px; padding: 48px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 48px; border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .project-links h2 { font-size: 2rem; } .project-links a { color: var(--accent); font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 600; } .project-links p { color: var(--muted); font-size: .85rem; }
  .source-links { display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  .case-nav { padding-block: 48px 96px; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; } .case-nav a { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .case-hero-grid, .project-links { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .case-hero-grid { margin-top: 40px; gap: 44px; } .case-body { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 760px); } .wide-section { padding-bottom: 80px; } .prose-section, .feature-section, .results-section { padding-block: 80px; } }
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .inline-metrics { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .inline-metrics div { border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); } .case-nav { flex-direction: column; } }
</style>
